VERDICT: PASS
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: R6
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope: internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go, internal/handlers/accounts/handlers_test.go,
internal/services/accounts/balance.go, internal/services/accounts/balance_test.go.

CLAIM UNDER TEST: "adding an anchor for a date that already has one now leaves exactly one
anchor for that date with the new amount authoritative, deterministically, and nothing else
changed." Verified TRUE. Detail below.

1. DAY-BOUNDARY / TIMEZONE ATTACK — did not break the claim.
   handlers.go's new sameCalendarDay(a,b) (line 320) and ledger/anchor.go's pre-existing
   sameDay(a,b) (already-accepted, out of scope) are semantically equivalent: both derive
   Y/M/D from each argument in that argument's OWN Location via time.Time.Date()/Year()/
   Month()/Day(). balance.go's dayOf (already-accepted) truncates in the value's own
   Location too. Since every anchor ever written by either the UI handler or the MCP tool
   is produced by time.Parse("2006-01-02", ...) — always midnight UTC — dayOf, sameDay and
   sameCalendarDay all agree in practice; verified with a standalone probe using an
   artificially non-UTC, non-midnight time.Time that .Date()-based comparison stays
   internally consistent (no divergence between the handler's day test and BalanceAt's).
   No case constructed where the UI would replace an anchor BalanceAt still treats as a
   different day, or vice versa.

2. TIE SURVIVING VIA ANOTHER PATH — brief's point about non-R6-written data. Confirmed:
   latestAnchorAtOrBefore's doc comment (balance.go:160-169) was updated and now explicitly
   states the FIRST-seen tie-break and explicitly scopes it to "data written outside those
   [UI/MCP] paths" — i.e., the worker documented the residual nondeterminism for hand-edited/
   imported data rather than claiming it's eliminated everywhere. TestLatestAnchorAtOrBefore_
   SameDayTieResolvesToFirstSeen (balance_test.go:547) exercises exactly that fallback and
   asserts which anchor wins (Note="first", Amount=1000.00) — not just that the call
   succeeds. Comment now matches code (mutated code reverted to "last seen" language would
   contradict `ad.After(dayOf(best.Date))`, and does not).

3. DOES THE DETERMINISM TEST PIN A VALUE? Yes.
   TestHandleAddAnchor_SameDayReplacementIsDeterministic (handlers_test.go:525) asserts
   Amount == 4210.55 (the second-added value) on every one of 50 iterations with a fresh
   store each time, plus BalanceAt() == 4210.55. This is a value assertion, not merely
   "no error" or "len==1".

4. R1 REGRESSION CHECK. go test -race -run 'LoadFailure|SaveFailure' -v
   ./internal/handlers/accounts/... — all 6 tests pass (load failure still 500 with
   byte-identical "Failed to load accounts: " prefix; save failure still 200 with inline
   Error). R6's edit sits inside the same Mutate callback shape R1 established and did not
   disturb the loaded-bool discipline.

5. DELETE-BY-DATE INVARIANT AFTER REPLACE. handleDeleteAnchor matches by a.Date.Equal(date)
   (exact timestamp), not calendar day. This is consistent with add: both add and delete
   parse dates via the identical time.Parse("2006-01-02", ...) call, so the anchor written
   by replace and the date looked up by delete are bit-identical time.Time values. Delete
   continues to work after a replace (not independently re-tested by R6, but the invariant
   is preserved by construction, and TestHandleDeleteAnchor_RemovesByDate already covers
   the general path). This asymmetry (delete = exact-equal, add/replace = calendar-day) is
   pre-existing and out of R6's stated scope.

6. NOTE LOSS ON REPLACE. Confirmed: the old anchor's Note is silently discarded (filtered
   out entirely, not merged or preserved). Brief explicitly permitted REPLACE as the
   preferred scope choice, so this is not a criterion failure. Nothing records the loss;
   there is no error/warning shown to the user on a successful replace (correct — it is not
   an error state), so ACCESSIBILITY.md's error-announcement rules do not apply here. Stated
   plainly per the task instructions: a user who had entered a Note on the stale anchor
   loses that note text with no confirmation or audit trail when they add a corrected
   anchor for the same day.

7. MUTATION TESTING (internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go, restored after each; working
   tree diffed byte-identical to pre-mutation state afterward):
   a. Revert to unconditional append + non-stable sort (the original defect) —
      TestHandleAddAnchor_ReplacesSameDayAnchor and
      TestHandleAddAnchor_SameDayReplacementIsDeterministic both FAIL as expected.
   b. Filter same-day anchors but drop the re-sort call — caught by the pre-existing
      TestHandleAddAnchor_KeepsAnchorsSortedByDate (FAIL as expected).
   c. Replace the calendar-day filter (sameCalendarDay) with exact timestamp equality
      (a.Date.Equal(date)) — NOT caught by any test; full suite still passes. This is a
      genuine test-coverage gap: no test constructs an anchor with a non-midnight
      time-of-day to distinguish calendar-day semantics from exact-equality semantics.
      However this does not indicate a defect in the DELIVERED code: reading
      sameCalendarDay directly (ay,am,ad := a.Date(); by,bm,bd := b.Date(); return
      ay==by && am==bm && ad==bd) confirms it correctly implements calendar-day
      comparison, not exact equality. The same blind spot exists in the already-accepted
      MCP-side sameDay/anchor_test.go (out of scope, pre-existing precedent), so this is
      not something R6 introduced or worsened, and it does not touch the specific defect
      (unstable-sort + first-seen tie-break) the brief describes and requires fixed.

8. BUILD/VET/TEST. go build ./... clean. go vet ./... clean. go test -race -count=1 ./...
   (full repo) exits 0, all packages ok including internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger (the
   known ~1/27 dashboard_parity_test.go flake, R9/out of scope, did not manifest this run).

Net: the specific defect described in the brief (unconditional append + unstable sort
producing nondeterministic, silently-stale anchors) is fixed, tested with value-pinning
assertions, and survives targeted mutation of the actual defect mechanics. The one
mutation that survived (day-boundary matching mode) targets a scenario outside the
delivered code's actual behavior and mirrors a pre-existing, out-of-scope precedent, so it
is reported as a finding, not treated as grounds for FAIL.
